Mumaks are very tough creatures when you first start to encounter them.
They should not be dealt with in melee if you can avoid it.

The following is a bit spoily, taken from the monster manual:
http://www.juiblex.co.uk/nethack/VernonSpoilers/MonsterManual/jaguar.html#jaguar

A jaguar is fast (speed 15). It has a base AC: 6 and three attacks:
Claw: 1d4
Claw: 1d4
Bite: 1d8

Similarly a lynx is fast (speed 15). It also has a base AC of 6 and
three attacks:
Claw: 1d4
Claw: 1d4
Bite: 1d10

So we're talking an average of around 9-10 points damage if all three
attacks hit. Possibly extra attacks per turn. Each of those attacks is
reduced by your AC though if it's negative, making the claw attacks
negligable.

But a Mumak has an armor class of 0. It has 2 attacks:
Butt: 4d12
Bite: 2d6

That averages to 31 points of damage with a maximum of 60! So, it's
harder to hit, and it hits much harder. Further more, your -3 AC
doesn't make as big a dent in each strike.

So yes, the Mumak's a tough customer, but it's only got a speed of 9.
Keep away from it, throw daggers, zap wands, and don't give it a chance
to hit you.



